{%- comment -%}
  PG RetroBox Story (2026-09-26): the selling sections of the PocketEra
  RetroBox page, cloned from sections/pg-pro2-story.liquid (the R36S Pro page)
  the way pg-r36h-story was: same layout, CSS and joined-module design, class
  prefix pg-p2s -> pg-rbs, data-p2s-* -> data-rbs-*, script
  pgx-pg-retrobox-story.js. Registered in templates/product.pg-retrobox.json
  only.

  THE RETROBOX IS A MINI TV CONSOLE, NOT A HANDHELD. It plugs into a TV over
  HDMI, has no screen and no battery, and comes in two editions (8-Bit: one
  wired controller; 16-Bit: two wired controllers), 30 built-in games each,
  save feature, USB 5V/2A power, 18-language menu. Every claim below is one of
  those. Nothing from the R36S pages is carried over (game counts, screen,
  chip, battery, microSD, firmware). No game or franchise is named anywhere.

  THE PIECES, EACH SEATED BY THE SCRIPT AT THE BOTTOM:
    1. BENEFITS (.pg-rbs-ben): four icons under the title: 30 Classic Games,
       Plugs Into Your TV, Save Your Progress, Ready Out of the Box.
    2. NO BUYER PHOTO STRIP. The Pro 2 builds one from Judge.me photo reviews;
       this product has no reviews of its own, so the strip, its "Loved by
       gamers" header (which printed the page's rating) and the "Customer
       Reviews" heading are left out entirely.
    3. THE STORY (#pg-rbs), one joined module:
       - Your childhood, upgraded: nostalgia headline and lead, with the 16-Bit
         desk photo;
       - Pick your era (replaces the Pro 2's "Do the math" used-handheld price
         comparison, which quoted a market price for a vintage handheld that
         has no honest equivalent here): the two editions as price cards, each
         a button that selects its tile and scrolls to it; the card of the
         selected tile is the lit one. Prices are printed from the variants.
         Its headline read "One Simple Price." while both editions were
         $119.99; they have been priced apart since 2026-09-26 (8-Bit $129.99,
         16-Bit $139.99, by request), so it reads "Choose Yours.", shorter
         than the old line, so it still fits one line on a 360px phone;
       - Under the hood: four spec tiles (30 Games, HD Output, Save Game,
         18 Languages);
       - In the box (replaces the Pro 2's accessory chapter, which sold the
         Max Bundle's cases): one card per edition with its studio photo;
       - Side by side: the two editions next to a used original console.
         Rows are only ones that are plainly true; no market prices.
       - Choose Your Edition button back up to the tiles.

  WHERE THINGS SIT. As on the Pro 2: the story is moved into #pgx under the
  product. The Pro 2 anchors it above the review grid (#pg-reviews); with no
  reviews on this page it is seated straight after .pgx-main instead, and
  You may also like (built late by pg-theme-css) follows it.

  PHOTOS are found by their alt text on the product ("8-Bit Edition - ...
  gaming desk" and so on), with the Shopify CDN file as the fallback, so the
  page still shows the right shot if the product's images are reordered.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if product -%}
